00:12:30.480 --> 00:12:33.040
But now, this way you can directly copy

00:12:33.040 --> 00:12:36.140
the very same element
different times, right?

00:12:36.140 --> 00:12:39.323
Now we can distribute it horizontally.

00:12:40.639 --> 00:12:43.310
And this already looks
quite decent, I'd say.

00:12:43.310 --> 00:12:45.683
So let's see, step one.

00:12:47.198 --> 00:12:49.990
Of course, this is now
also supposed to be,

00:12:49.990 --> 00:12:51.993
let's maybe re-mark this, in Arial.

00:12:54.650 --> 00:12:56.910
So let's just, for the sake of time,

00:12:56.910 --> 00:12:58.773
insert the text boxes here.

00:12:59.750 --> 00:13:03.493
Of course, you can also
copy it in the same way.

00:13:04.490 --> 00:13:09.073
If we align this here to
the left and distribute it,

00:13:10.230 --> 00:13:13.320
it's all nice and evenly spread out,

00:13:13.320 --> 00:13:16.893
and of course you would know
how to insert such a line.

00:13:22.860 --> 00:13:25.343
Now you can do some basic
formatting of the line.

00:13:27.030 --> 00:13:29.793
Let's put it at one-point weight,

00:13:34.307 --> 00:13:36.023
and we make it dotted.

00:13:38.360 --> 00:13:40.813
So this way, it's all
aligned in the proper way.

00:13:42.162 --> 00:13:44.660
And now we have a chart that
more or less looks like this.

00:13:44.660 --> 00:13:46.230
Now, probably, this is a bit too close

00:13:46.230 --> 00:13:48.470
to the bullets on the
right, so we'll shift

00:13:48.470 --> 00:13:51.280
them over a bit more to the left.

00:13:51.280 --> 00:13:54.740
And now, you have a properly nice and fine

00:13:54.740 --> 00:13:57.400
process chart, just the way we wanted to.

00:13:57.400 --> 00:13:59.460
Of course, sometimes you need a bit more

00:13:59.460 --> 00:14:01.920
complex shapes that might not directly

00:14:01.920 --> 00:14:04.120
come out of PowerPoint out of the box.

00:14:04.120 --> 00:14:07.470
if you look here in the Shapes bar,

00:14:07.470 --> 00:14:09.330
and sometimes you might be inclined

00:14:09.330 --> 00:14:12.420
to see that, actually,
things like that exist

00:14:12.420 --> 00:14:15.707
in the Smart Art section,
so you might ask yourself,

00:14:15.707 --> 00:14:17.447
"Well, how can I actually create these

00:14:17.447 --> 00:14:21.240
"more complex shapes
without using Smart Arts?"

00:14:21.240 --> 00:14:22.660
So let's try that out.

00:14:22.660 --> 00:14:24.220
Let's create a new page here.

00:14:24.220 --> 00:14:26.120
I'm deleting all of this.

00:14:26.120 --> 00:14:29.060
And let's, for instance,
say, well, let's look,

00:14:29.060 --> 00:14:30.550
what looks maybe a bit more fancy?

00:14:30.550 --> 00:14:34.500
For instance, these circles
with different cutlines,

00:14:34.500 --> 00:14:36.600
and through it, it then shows,

00:14:36.600 --> 00:14:39.160
for instance, if the glass is empty

00:14:39.160 --> 00:14:41.510
or half-full or completely
full of whatever

00:14:41.510 --> 00:14:43.303
this is supposed to symbolize, right?

00:14:43.303 --> 00:14:45.080
Now, this is supposed to look like,

00:14:45.080 --> 00:14:47.280
and, interestingly
enough, here, now you can

00:14:47.280 --> 00:14:50.180
actually just make it
bigger, but let's imagine

00:14:50.180 --> 00:14:52.710
we want to create circles like that.

00:14:52.710 --> 00:14:55.600
How would you want to do that
without using Smart Arts,

00:14:55.600 --> 00:14:58.680
because we all know that
something exactly like that

00:14:58.680 --> 00:15:01.240
just doesn't exist here
in the Shapes section?

00:15:01.240 --> 00:15:04.370
So let's delete that, and
you always need to realize

00:15:04.370 --> 00:15:06.830
that these more complex
shapes are usually created

00:15:06.830 --> 00:15:10.210
out of more basic shapes, like circles.

00:15:10.210 --> 00:15:14.160
So if we create a circle
here, we again format it,

00:15:14.160 --> 00:15:16.680
now we can probably
easily create a version

00:15:16.680 --> 00:15:19.690
in which the circle was full.

00:15:19.690 --> 00:15:21.380
Probably it looks something like that.

00:15:21.380 --> 00:15:22.793
Let's give it another color.

00:15:24.700 --> 00:15:29.293
And now we can center it in here properly.

00:15:30.240 --> 00:15:33.440
Now this is something like the circle

00:15:33.440 --> 00:15:34.630
that we saw earlier.

00:15:34.630 --> 00:15:36.790
But now, what do we need to do in order

00:15:36.790 --> 00:15:38.270
to cut it in half, for instance,

00:15:38.270 --> 00:15:40.657
to make this inner part appear in half?

00:15:40.657 --> 00:15:42.950
And here is now a section that's really

00:15:42.950 --> 00:15:45.010
handy and useful in PowerPoint

00:15:45.010 --> 00:15:47.050
that maybe you already know, or maybe not,

00:15:47.050 --> 00:15:48.650
and this is called merged shapes.

00:15:48.650 --> 00:15:50.610
And with merged shapes, you can create

00:15:50.610 --> 00:15:53.260
some more complex shape forms for

00:15:53.260 --> 00:15:55.170
your use in PowerPoint.

00:15:55.170 --> 00:15:57.190
'Cause now, what we actually need is to

00:15:57.190 --> 00:16:00.220
kind of cut out the
upper half of the circle,

00:16:00.220 --> 00:16:02.340
or some portion of the circle, in order

00:16:02.340 --> 00:16:05.050
to create a look where
this circle is not whole,

00:16:05.050 --> 00:16:07.420
but actually divided into different parts.

00:16:07.420 --> 00:16:11.180
And we can do that by subtracting

00:16:11.180 --> 00:16:13.700
another form from the circle form.

00:16:13.700 --> 00:16:16.560
So let's now just create a second form,

00:16:16.560 --> 00:16:18.944
a square, which only purpose it is

00:16:18.944 --> 00:16:22.530
to subtract something
from this lower form.

00:16:22.530 --> 00:16:24.460
Now you see that I aligned it in a way

00:16:24.460 --> 00:16:26.840
that this cuts the circle in half,

00:16:26.840 --> 00:16:28.860
or would cut the circle in half.

00:16:28.860 --> 00:16:32.180
And now I need to select these two shapes.

00:16:32.180 --> 00:16:33.980
For the subtract function, of course,

00:16:33.980 --> 00:16:36.580
it's always important
which shape gets subtracted

00:16:36.580 --> 00:16:39.480
from which shape, so let's
me do it the wrong way

00:16:39.480 --> 00:16:42.780
so that we first select this
shape and second this shape.

00:16:42.780 --> 00:16:46.917
If I now go into Shape
Format, go into Merged Shapes,

00:16:46.917 --> 00:16:51.090
and then go into Subtract, now the circle

00:16:51.090 --> 00:16:54.860
is subtracted from the rectangle.

00:16:54.860 --> 00:16:56.050
But this is, of course, not what we want.

00:16:56.050 --> 00:16:58.541
We first need to be selecting the form

00:16:58.541 --> 00:17:01.430
from which something is to be subtracted,

00:17:01.430 --> 00:17:04.390
and then second, we select the form

00:17:04.390 --> 00:17:06.430
which is being subtracted.

00:17:06.430 --> 00:17:09.560
And if you do it in this
way and if you Merge Shape

00:17:09.560 --> 00:17:13.070
and then Subtract, then
you get the half-circle

00:17:13.070 --> 00:17:15.600
just the way that you
want it, pretty much.

00:17:15.600 --> 00:17:19.387
So let's insert it here, and
I'll delete the other shape,

00:17:19.387 --> 00:17:21.560
and this is exactly what you want.

00:17:21.560 --> 00:17:23.260
And now, of course, if you want to do it

00:17:23.260 --> 00:17:25.470
in a way that's not cutting it in half,

00:17:25.470 --> 00:17:27.890
but maybe even a bit more than in half,

00:17:27.890 --> 00:17:30.450
you can just put it like this,

00:17:30.450 --> 00:17:35.220
and then you Shape Format,
Merge, and Subtract

00:17:35.220 --> 00:17:39.240
to now get this even smaller
subpart of the circle

00:17:39.240 --> 00:17:41.270
that might want to insert here.

00:17:41.270 --> 00:17:45.020
And with this function
of merging, subtracting,

00:17:45.020 --> 00:17:47.310
and other operations that
you have with the shapes,

00:17:47.310 --> 00:17:50.417
you can create very
complex forms if you want.

00:17:50.417 --> 00:17:52.860
And just to go over, then, what do these

00:17:52.860 --> 00:17:56.110
different things do, here
you can go into Union,

00:17:56.110 --> 00:17:58.853
which gives you here the very same shape,

00:18:00.020 --> 00:18:03.390
you can go into Combine, where
it subtracts the upper part

00:18:03.390 --> 00:18:06.120
but still leaves you with the lower part,

00:18:06.120 --> 00:18:11.107
you can go with Fragment, which now

00:18:12.460 --> 00:18:15.323
gives you the subpaths of
all these different shape,

00:18:17.090 --> 00:18:20.240
and you can go, Subtract, we already did,

00:18:20.240 --> 00:18:22.250
but Intersect will give you only the part

00:18:22.250 --> 00:18:24.010
that's actually covered by both shapes,

00:18:24.010 --> 00:18:25.930
so that'd be this one.

00:18:25.930 --> 00:18:28.280
So I hope that you can see that the things

00:18:28.280 --> 00:18:29.850
that you can create, the
things that you can do,

00:18:29.850 --> 00:18:32.260
are pretty much limitless,
and you can create

00:18:32.260 --> 00:18:33.970
really complex shapes here.

00:18:33.970 --> 00:18:36.520
So, to sum up, in professional contexts,

00:18:36.520 --> 00:18:39.950
Smart Arts are just not
being used very often.

00:18:39.950 --> 00:18:42.304
If you do use them, at least in a strategy

00:18:42.304 --> 00:18:44.940
consulting environment, you will be told

00:18:44.940 --> 00:18:47.200
by your project lead or by your partner

00:18:47.200 --> 00:18:49.480
that, please, you do not use Smart Arts

00:18:49.480 --> 00:18:52.145
because it just lets
you look unprofessional,

00:18:52.145 --> 00:18:55.220
even if you are somehow
tweaking it (laughs)

00:18:55.220 --> 00:18:56.781
and spend lots of time,
you might manage to

00:18:56.781 --> 00:18:59.780
get a Smart Art look the
way that you want it.

00:18:59.780 --> 00:19:01.650
And the easiest and most forward way,

00:19:01.650 --> 00:19:03.450
from my perspective, is just to avoid

00:19:03.450 --> 00:19:06.950
that entirely, create
the shapes, the graphics,

00:19:06.950 --> 00:19:10.140
that you want to use manually by combining

00:19:10.140 --> 00:19:13.760
the different subelements,
subforms, subshapes

00:19:13.760 --> 00:19:16.270
that you can create here
just in a way that you

00:19:16.270 --> 00:19:18.510
want to, then you can create perfectly

00:19:18.510 --> 00:19:21.810
proficient-looking slides,
like process diagrams,

00:19:21.810 --> 00:19:23.661
in the way that we did
it, and also by combining

00:19:23.661 --> 00:19:28.090
different forms using the
merge shapes functionality,

00:19:28.090 --> 00:19:31.410
you can also create more
complex forms which might not

00:19:31.410 --> 00:19:34.540
be existent in PowerPoint out of the box.

00:19:34.540 --> 00:19:36.540
So thank you for watching,
and I hope all of that

00:19:36.540 --> 00:19:37.650
made sense to you.

00:19:37.650 --> 00:19:39.450
So let me know, what do you think?

00:19:39.450 --> 00:19:42.030
Do you use Smart Arts in your
PowerPoint presentations,

00:19:42.030 --> 00:19:45.690
and did I actually convince
you to not use them anymore?

00:19:45.690 --> 00:19:47.910
If not, well, I guess
that's also a valid opinion.

00:19:47.910 --> 00:19:49.820
Just let me know what your thoughts are,

00:19:49.820 --> 00:19:52.540
and I'm also happy to answer
any comments you might have.

